  "contexts": [
    {
      "id": "shift",
      "track": "section-b",
      "markdown": "Let a be an array of integers of length n, where n ≥ 1. A left circular shift on the array is defined as setting a[0], a[1], · · · , a[n-3], a[n-2], a[n-1] to be a[1], a[2], · · · , a[n-2], a[n-1], a[0] simultaneously.\n\nFor example, if n = 5 and a = [1,2,3,4,5]. a would become [2,3,4,5,1] after one left circular shift."
    },
    {
      "id": "query",
      "track": "section-b",
      "markdown": "Given an array (NOT necessarily sorted) A[0..N − 1] of N integers, and an array of queries Q[0..M − 1]. For query Q[j], output either the smallest index i (0 ≤ i < N) such that A[i] > Q[j], or N if no such index exists. You must output the answers to the queries in order."
    },
    {
      "id": "coins",
      "track": "section-b",
      "markdown": "In Heung Shing, there are only four denominations of coins, $1, $2, $5 and $10. This results in multiple ways to pay the same amount without change. For example, there are 6 ways to pay $7 without change:\n\n| Way | Coins used |\n|---:|---|\n| 1 | $1 + $1 + $1 + $2 + $2 |\n| 2 | $2 + $5 |\n| 3 | $1 + $1 + $1 + $1 + $1 + $1 + $1 |\n| 4 | $1 + $1 + $5 |\n| 5 | $1 + $2 + $2 + $2 |\n| 6 | $1 + $1 + $1 + $1 + $1 + $2 |\n\nAlice has already completed a function CountNo1(n) that calculates the number of ways to pay $n without change, using $2, $5 and $10 coins only. The function signature is as follows.\n\n```cpp\nint CountNo1 (int n);\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "bitshift",
      "track": "section-b",
      "markdown": "Consider the following function:\n\n```cpp\nint f(int x) {\n    int a = 1 << x;\n    int b = 1;\n    int res = 0;\n    while (a) {\n        res += a * b;\n        a >>= 1;\n        ++b;\n    }\n    res += b;\n    return res;\n}\n```\n\nYou CANNOT call other functions in your answers to parts (a) and (b), including f."
    }
  ],
